        <p>Chart week is the Billboard Hot 100 week that contains February 1 that year.</p>
        <p>Week of run says which week of the song's time at number one that was, like 3 of 4.</p>
        <p>Chart weeks and weeks at number one come from this site's weekly Hot 100 file, and the story lines are credited in Sources.</p>

        <h2 id="answer">The Short Answer: Who Was on Top Each Year</h2>
        <p>For February 1 in music history, a different record was number one in every year from 1970 to 1979.</p>
        <p>That is the Hot 100 chart week that includes February 1.</p>
        <p>Here is what the ten weeks show at a glance:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>The ten songs spent {{TOTAL_WEEKS}} weeks at number one in total.</li>
          <li>"Le Freak" had the most weeks, with six, followed by "American Pie" with four.</li>
          <li>Two songs had three weeks each: "Knock Three Times" and "Baby Come Back."</li>
          <li>{{N_FIRST}} of the ten songs had just one week at number one, and February 1 fell in that week for all of them.</li>
          <li>{{N_MID}} were somewhere in the middle of a longer run, and {{N_LAST}} was in the final week of its run.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Want Your Own Week?</h3>
        <p>The <a href="/pages/birthday-number-one">Birthday #1 Song Finder</a> does this for any 1970s birthday, not only February 1 in music history.</p>
        <p>Enter the date and it shows the song that was on top that week.</p>

        <h2 id="method">How the February 1 Week Was Picked</h2>
        <p>Billboard chart weeks do not line up with the calendar, so there is no single week called February 1.</p>
        <p>This February 1 in music history page uses the weekly Hot 100 week that contains February 1 each year.</p>
        <p>In nine of the ten years, that week began in late January:</p>
        <ul>
{{DATE_LIST}}
        </ul>

        <h3>Where the Numbers Come From</h3>
        <p>Chart weeks and weeks at number one come from the weekly Hot 100 file on this site.</p>
        <p>The story line for each song is a fact from that song's Wikipedia page.</p>
        <p>Anything that could not be confirmed against those sources was left out.</p>
        <p>Nine of the ten runs were unbroken.</p>
        <p>For those, "week of run" counts the weeks since the song first reached number one.</p>
        <p>One run was split by other songs, twice: "Le Freak" in 1979.</p>
        <p>For that one, it counts only the weeks the song itself held number one.</p>

        <h2 id="early">February 1 in Music History, 1970 to 1972: A Child Lead Singer, a Group Called Dawn and an Eight-Minute Epic</h2>
        <p>February 1 in music history opened the decade with the Jackson 5's first Motown single.</p>
        <p>Next came a studio group fronted by Tony Orlando.</p>
        <p>Then came Don McLean's eight-minute song about the day the music died.</p>

        <h3>1970: "I Want You Back" by The Jackson 5</h3>
        <p>"I Want You Back" was the first song written and produced by the Motown team known as The Corporation.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says it was first considered for Gladys Knight &amp; the Pips and later for Diana Ross.</p>
        <p>Motown released it in October 1969, from the debut album Diana Ross Presents The Jackson 5.</p>
        <p>The lead vocal was sung by an 11-year-old Michael Jackson.</p>
        <p>In 1999 it was inducted into the Grammy Hall of Fame.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows one week at number one, the chart week of January 31, 1970.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains February 1 was the first and only one.</p>
        <p>The song is number 28 on the 1970 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>More of the group is on the <a href="/blog/artists/jackson-5-songs/">Jackson 5 songs</a> page.</p>

        <h3>1971: "Knock Three Times" by Dawn</h3>
        <p>Irwin Levine and Larry Brown wrote "Knock Three Times."</p>
        <p>Hank Medress and Dave Appell produced it, and it came out in November 1970 on the album Candida.</p>
        <p>Tony Orlando sang lead, but the single was credited simply to Dawn.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says the name came from Lisa Dawn Wax, the daughter of Bell Records executive Steve Wax.</p>
        <p>Outside the US, it also reached number one on the UK Singles Chart.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows three weeks at number one, from January 23 to February 6, 1971.</p>
        <p>George Harrison's "My Sweet Lord" was on top just before it, and the Osmonds' "One Bad Apple" came next.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains February 1 was the second of three.</p>
        <p>The song is number 10 on the 1971 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>

        <h3>1972: "American Pie" by Don McLean</h3>
        <p>Don McLean wrote "American Pie," Ed Freeman produced it, and it came out in 1971 on the album of the same name.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says "the day the music died" refers to the plane crash that killed Buddy Holly, The Big Bopper and Ritchie Valens.</p>
        <p>That crash was on February 3, 1959, two days after February 1 on the calendar.</p>
        <p>McLean was a 13-year-old newspaper delivery boy in New Rochelle, New York, at the time.</p>
        <p>At 8 minutes and 42 seconds, it held the record for almost 50 years as the longest song to reach number one.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows four weeks at number one, from January 15 to February 5, 1972.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains February 1 was the third of four.</p>
        <p>The song is number 3 on the 1972 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>The full story is in the <a href="/blog/songs/don-mclean-american-pie/">Don McLean American Pie</a> post.</p>

        <h2 id="middle">February 1 in Music History, 1973 to 1975: A Clavinet Riff, a Beatle's Cover and a Comeback</h2>
        <p>February 1 in music history in the middle of the decade brought a Stevie Wonder funk record.</p>
        <p>A Ringo Starr cover of a 1960 hit followed.</p>
        <p>Then Neil Sedaka topped the Hot 100 for the first time since 1962.</p>

        <h3>1973: "Superstition" by Stevie Wonder</h3>
        <p>Stevie Wonder wrote "Superstition" and released it as the lead single from Talking Book, his fifteenth studio album.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says Jeff Beck came up with the opening drum beat.</p>
        <p>Beck released his own version in March 1973.</p>
        <p>Wonder played the riff on a Hohner Clavinet model C.</p>
        <p>The song earned Wonder two Grammys.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia's list of 1973 number ones shows it on top for one week, the January 27 issue.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data agrees, with one week at number one, the chart week of January 27, 1973.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains February 1 was the first and only one.</p>
        <p>The song is number 26 on the 1973 year-end Hot 100, and the <a href="/blog/songs/superstition-stevie-wonder/">Superstition</a> post has more.</p>

        <h3>1974: "You're Sixteen" by Ringo Starr</h3>
        <p>The Sherman Brothers wrote "You're Sixteen."</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says Johnny Burnette's original peaked at number eight on the Hot 100 in December 1960.</p>
        <p>Ringo Starr covered it in 1973 on his album Ringo, with Richard Perry producing.</p>
        <p>Paul McCartney sang a "kazoo" vocal solo, and Harry Nilsson sang backing vocals.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says Starr's version reached number one on the Hot 100 in January 1974.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows one week at number one, the chart week of January 26, 1974.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains February 1 was the first and only one.</p>
        <p>The song is number 31 on the 1974 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>

        <h3>1975: "Laughter in the Rain" by Neil Sedaka</h3>
        <p>Neil Sedaka composed "Laughter in the Rain," with lyrics by Phil Cody.</p>
        <p>Sedaka and Robert Appere produced it, for the album Sedaka's Back.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says English singer Peter Gordeno released the song first, in May 1974.</p>
        <p>Sedaka's version came out in the US on Elton John's Rocket label.</p>
        <p>It was his first single to top the Hot 100 since 1962.</p>
        <p>It also spent two weeks at the top of the Adult Contemporary chart.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows one week at number one, the chart week of February 1, 1975.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains February 1 was the first and only one.</p>
        <p>The song is number 9 on the 1975 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>

        <h2 id="late">February 1 in Music History, 1976 to 1979: A Scream Legend, a Movie Theme, a Breakup Song and a Three-Run Record</h2>
        <p>February 1 in music history closed the decade with an Ohio Players funk hit and a movie theme.</p>
        <p>A soft rock breakup song followed.</p>
        <p>Then came a disco record that reached number one three separate times.</p>

        <h3>1976: "Love Rollercoaster" by Ohio Players</h3>
        <p>The Ohio Players first released "Love Rollercoaster" on their 1975 album Honey.</p>
        <p>Seven writers share the credit.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says it was a number one US hit in January 1976 and also topped the Hot Soul Singles chart.</p>
        <p>A persistent urban legend says a scream on the record was a person being murdered while the tape rolled.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia quotes Jimmy "Diamond" Williams, who says Billy Beck does "one of those inhaling-type screeches."</p>
        <p>Red Hot Chili Peppers released a cover as a single in November 1996.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows one week at number one, the chart week of January 31, 1976.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains February 1 was the first and only one.</p>
        <p>The song is number 30 on the 1976 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>

        <h3>1977: "Car Wash" by Rose Royce</h3>
        <p>Former Motown producer Norman Whitfield wrote and arranged "Car Wash" as the theme of the 1976 film Car Wash.</p>
        <p>Rose Royce was a disco and funk backing band that Whitfield signed to his own label in 1975.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says inspiration finally struck while Whitfield was watching a game of basketball.</p>
        <p>He wrote the first draft on a paper bag from a fried chicken restaurant.</p>
        <p>It reached number one on both the Billboard pop and R&amp;B charts and sold 2 million copies.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows one week at number one, the chart week of January 29, 1977.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains February 1 was the first and only one.</p>
        <p>The song is number 26 on the 1977 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>

        <h3>1978: "Baby Come Back" by Player</h3>
        <p>Peter Beckett and J. C. Crowley, the founders of Player, wrote and performed "Baby Come Back."</p>
        <p>Dennis Lambert and Brian Potter produced it.</p>
        <p>It came out in late 1977 as the lead single from the band's self-titled debut album.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says it was written after two of the band members had broken up with their girlfriends.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows three weeks at number one, from January 14 to January 28, 1978.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains February 1 was the third and last.</p>
        <p>The Bee Gees' "Stayin' Alive" took over the next week.</p>
        <p>The song is number 7 on the 1978 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>

        <h3>1979: "Le Freak" by Chic</h3>
        <p>Bernard Edwards and Nile Rodgers of Chic wrote and produced "Le Freak."</p>
        <p>Atlantic Records released it in September 1978 as the first single from the album C'est Chic.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says the song commemorates Studio 54 and its notoriously long customer waiting lines.</p>
        <p>It was the first song to reach number one on the Hot 100 three separate times.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows December 9, 1978, then December 23 and 30, then January 20 and 27 and February 3, 1979.</p>
        <p>"You Don't Bring Me Flowers" by Barbra Streisand and Neil Diamond, then the Bee Gees' "Too Much Heaven," held the gaps.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains February 1 was the fifth of six.</p>
        <p>The song is number 3 on the 1979 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>The first week of that run tops the <a href="/blog/trivia/december-12-in-music-history/">December 12 in music history</a> list.</p>

